1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:09,460
So if you are a director general listening to this, uh, or a senior government official preparing your minister, this deep dive is a policy briefing designed specifically for you.

2
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:15,560
We are looking at why foundational digital identity systems, well, why they must be built just once.

3
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:16,020
Right.

4
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:20,100
And that really is the core premise here, the golden rule, if you will.

5
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:24,540
I mean, an identity system is just incredibly costly to build and run.

6
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:27,740
A country should build it once for every service to use.

7
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:31,880
Because if your ministry builds its own, uh, it pays those costs a second time.

8
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:35,640
Exactly. But, I mean, it is a really common temptation, right?

9
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:42,540
Like a sector, say a ministry of education wanting to know its learners, uh, they are tempted to issue their own ID.

10
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:51,100
But what does that actually entail? Like, why shouldn't a specific ministry treat an identity system like an ordinary registration screen?

11
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:55,000
Well, the GovStack identity specification is actually very clear on this.

12
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:59,680
A foundational identity is the base for every digital interaction.

13
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:07,440
It is not just a form. Right. Because it is highly valuable, it attracts attackers, so it demands the absolute highest security.

14
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:10,400
Enrolling people well requires time,

15
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:13,860
uh, verifying documents, and heavily trained staff.

16
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,080
Making it a full national task.

17
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,200
Yes, a national task, not just a side project for one ministry.

18
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:27,580
Okay, so if a ministry attempts this alone, the finance ministry really needs to know the exact financial burden being duplicated.

19
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:32,280
What specific cost categories will a ministry duplicate if they decide to go it alone?

20
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:35,620
So based on a twenty eighteen World Bank study,

21
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:39,600
we know over ninety percent of startup costs fall into six categories.

22
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,500
Oh, wow, ninety percent. Which ones?

23
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:44,720
Human resources, the identity credential,

24
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:46,820
central IT infrastructure,

25
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:52,200
physical establishments, enrollment IT, and information campaigns.

26
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:55,340
And what about when it is actually running?

27
00:01:56,020 --> 00:02:00,360
Once running, human resources swallow over eighty percent of the annual cost.

28
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:02,460
Over eighty percent just on staff.

29
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:10,780
Yes. Furthermore, the physical credential, you know, the card itself, ranges from three percent to over forty percent of the total cost.

30
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:16,400
And procurement methods can swing overall cost by twenty-five percent to over a hundred percent.

31
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:22,880
So to make these abstract costs concrete, let's look at how this plays out in the demonstration country of Progresa.

32
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:27,080
If Progresa's Ministry of Education ran its own learner identity,

33
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:31,340
which specific burdens would they carry compared to using the national system?

34
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:39,100
Well, if the education ministry built it, they would duplicate staff, printed cards, central systems, offices, kits, and campaigns.

35
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:43,080
Things become duplicated, siloed, and costly to change.

36
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:47,460
But by using the National Identity Authority, PNIA, instead...

37
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:51,420
The ministry carries only the work of connecting its service and testing it.

38
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:54,320
The reference architecture expects exactly this.

39
00:02:54,920 --> 00:03:01,440
It expects reusing the existing system behind a services façade, so applicants simply sign in with their foundational identity.

40
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:07,880
So you avoid having systems fragmented or data rekeyed or being locked into one supplier.

41
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:11,240
Identity costs a great deal to build and more to run.

42
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,300
A country pays that cost once.

43
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:16,180
A ministry that builds its own pays it again.

44
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:18,520
Sources are in the video description.
